window.EXAM=[{"id":"u1","n":1,"t":"Lifestyles","a":["hectic","mindful","priority","deliberately","overwhelmed","cut back","clutter","savour","commitment","recharge"],"b":["screen time","notification","distraction","addictive","willpower","boundary","constantly","disconnect","productivity","urge","scroll","habit"],"box":["recharge","hectic","deliberately","overwhelmed","savour","cut back","priorities","mindful","clutter","commitment"],"gap":[["After a busy month, I need a holiday to ___.","recharge"],["My life has been really ___ since I started two jobs.","hectic"],["She ___ turned off notifications to concentrate.","deliberately"],["Too many emails made him feel ___.","overwhelmed"],["I try to ___ every bite of a good meal.","savour"],["We should ___ on plastic to help the planet.","cut back"],["Health and sleep are my top ___.","priorities"],["Being ___ means noticing the present moment.","mindful"],["A tidy room has less ___ and feels calmer.","clutter"],["Going to the gym three times a week is a big ___.","commitment"]],"rt":"The Art of Slowing Down","rp":["Modern life can feel incredibly hectic. Many people wake up to a long list of tasks, rush through the day, and fall into bed exhausted. Between work, study, social activities, and constant notifications, there is often very little time left to simply pause. People may feel that they always need to be doing something, even during their free time. In recent years, however, a growing number of people have decided to cut back on this fast pace. They are part of what is known as the \"slow living\" movement.","Slow living does not mean being lazy or doing everything slowly. Instead, it means being more mindful about how we spend our time and energy. People who choose this lifestyle try to focus on their real priorities rather than filling every moment with activity. They may think more carefully about which activities are important to them and which ones simply make their days busier. They deliberately make time to savour simple pleasures, such as cooking a meal, reading a book, or talking with a friend. For some people, this can also mean enjoying a quiet morning, taking a walk, or spending an evening without a busy schedule.","Anna, a graphic designer from Berlin, felt completely overwhelmed by her schedule two years ago. She often felt that one busy day simply followed another, leaving her with little energy for herself. \"I was always tired and never happy,\" she explains. \"So, I decided to change. I reduced my working hours and started saying no to commitments I didn't enjoy.\" Now she has more time to recharge and feels much healthier. She has also discovered that having more free time allows her to enjoy ordinary parts of her day that she used to overlook.","Not everyone finds it easy. Marco, a student, admits that slowing down is difficult when your phone is full of messages and your calendar is cluttered with deadlines. Like many students, he has to balance different responsibilities and often feels pressure to keep up with everything. \"The idea is wonderful,\" he says, \"but in reality, university life doesn't always allow it.\" Although he likes the idea of having a calmer lifestyle, he knows that changing his routine is not always simple.","Experts say that even small changes can help. Turning off your phone during meals, or spending ten minutes outside, can create a better balance. Other simple habits, such as taking short breaks or leaving some free time between activities, may also help people feel less rushed. The goal is not to completely change your lifestyle overnight. Instead, people can begin by making small adjustments to the way they use their time. In a world that never seems to stop, learning to slow down may be one of the most valuable skills of all."],"rq":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the text?","o":["To explain the main reasons behind the increasingly fast pace of modern life","To describe a lifestyle choice that emphasizes doing less while focusing on quality","To encourage people to give up their careers and stop working completely","To examine the differences between the lifestyles of two German cities"],"a":1},{"q":"In the text, \"cut back on\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["reduce the amount of something","increase the amount of something","completely eliminate something from your life","make greater use of something"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did Anna change her lifestyle?","o":["She was unexpectedly forced to leave her job.","She decided to start a new life after moving to Berlin.","She had become increasingly exhausted and dissatisfied with her life.","She was advised by her doctor to adopt a slower lifestyle."],"a":2},{"q":"What can we understand about Marco's opinion?","o":["He is convinced that slow living is ultimately harmful.","He finds the concept appealing, although he struggles to put it into practice.","He has successfully transformed every aspect of his lifestyle.","He is largely indifferent to the idea and its potential benefits."],"a":1},{"q":"According to experts, what can help people find balance?","o":["Giving up higher education and focusing on personal interests","Avoiding daily responsibilities and spending most of the day resting","Introducing manageable changes, such as having meals without using phones","Purchasing more products in order to make everyday life more convenient"],"a":2}],"who":{"p":["Anna","Marco","Experts"],"q":[["Even small changes in our daily routines can make a real difference.","Experts"],["I stopped agreeing to things that I didn't really enjoy or find meaningful.","Anna"],["Slow living is an appealing idea, but it can be difficult for students to put it into practice.","Marco"],["I now feel much healthier and have more energy than I did before.","Anna"],["Turning off your phone during meals can help create a healthier balance in everyday life.","Experts"]]},"lq":[{"q":"Who is the expert in the interview?","o":["Tom Bradley, a university student","The radio presenter of Modern Living","A psychologist who designs apps","Dr Elena Frost, a well-being coach"],"a":3},{"q":"What is the interview mainly about?","o":["Finding the best apps for university students","Explaining how apps are designed and sold","Comparing how adults and students use phones","Building a healthier relationship with screens"],"a":3},{"q":"How many hours a day does the average adult spend on screens?","o":["around seven","around six","around nine","around five"],"a":0},{"q":"According to Elena, what is the real problem?","o":["Having too many apps on our devices","Checking our phones repeatedly throughout the day","Spending too much money on new technology","Depending on a fast internet connection"],"a":1},{"q":"\"I don't have the willpower to stop\" means Tom:","o":["is unsure how to change his habit.","finds it difficult to control the habit.","doesn't see any reason to change.","has already managed to break the habit."],"a":1},{"q":"What can we understand about Tom by the end?","o":["Changing his habits was too difficult for him.","Making one small change made a noticeable difference.","He remained unconvinced by Elena's advice.","He decided to stop using his phone completely."],"a":1}],"lwho":{"p":["Elena","Tom"],"q":[["Apps are designed to keep us engaged for as long as possible.","Elena"],["I find it difficult to break the habit of checking my phone.","Tom"],["Leaving your phone outside the bedroom can lead to better sleep.","Elena"],["Since I changed my screen habits, I've become more productive.","Tom"],["You don't have to cut out screens completely — the ultimate goal is to find a healthier balance.","Elena"]]},"au":"audio/u1.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"In the first paragraph, \"hectic\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["extremely tiring because of physical work","noisy and crowded with too many people","very busy and full of activity","exciting and full of unexpected surprises"],"a":2},{"q":"According to the text, what does slow living mean?","o":["Choosing fewer but more expensive activities to enjoy","Being more mindful about how we spend our time and energy","Doing every task slowly to avoid making mistakes","Spending free time alone rather than with other people"],"a":1},{"q":"What did Anna do to change her life?","o":["She quit her job and started a smaller design business.","She cut down on her social life to focus on her career.","She reduced her working hours and said no to some commitments.","She asked her employer for a longer holiday every year."],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Marco find slowing down difficult?","o":["His friends do not support his plans to change his routine.","He has to work long hours at a part-time job.","He believes the idea of slow living is unrealistic for everyone.","His phone is full of messages and his calendar is full of deadlines."],"a":3},{"q":"According to the last paragraph, what is the goal?","o":["To completely change your lifestyle within a short time","To find a job with fewer working hours","To make small adjustments to the way you use your time","To reduce your use of all technology"],"a":2},{"q":"Which suggestion do the experts make?","o":["Switching off the phone for a whole weekend","Keeping a diary of every activity during the day","Spending ten minutes outside","Planning each hour of the day in advance"],"a":2}],"lq2":[{"q":"What is the first thing Tom touches in the morning?","o":["His laptop","His alarm clock","The television remote","His phone"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Elena advise clients to do about the bedroom?","o":["Keep the phone out of the bedroom","Switch the phone to silent mode at night","Use the phone only for emergency calls","Charge the phone far from the bed"],"a":0},{"q":"What did Tom buy to replace his phone alarm?","o":["A traditional alarm clock","A smartwatch with an alarm","A radio with a timer","A second, cheaper phone"],"a":0},{"q":"How were the first few nights without the phone for Tom?","o":["Surprisingly easy","Quite boring","Unusually quiet and relaxing","Really hard"],"a":3},{"q":"What does Elena say is the ultimate goal?","o":["Finding a healthy balance","Using no screens at all","Spending screen time only on work","Having less screen time than other adults"],"a":0},{"q":"According to Elena, why do notifications affect our focus?","o":["They make us feel anxious about our work.","They pull our attention away from what we are doing.","They arrive mostly when we are busy.","They remind us about tasks we have forgotten."],"a":1}],"ph":[{"q":"I'd put \"no phone during meals\" _____ because it's the easiest habit.","o":["first","before","early","one"],"a":0},{"q":"I think a screen-free evening would be _____ because I use my phone to relax.","o":["harder","hardly","hardness","hardening"],"a":0},{"q":"I'm not _____ that would work for me because I need my phone for work.","o":["sure","agree","think","want"],"a":0},{"q":"We could make it easier _____ turning off non-essential notifications.","o":["by","with","for","to"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u2","n":2,"t":"Makers","a":["invention","entrepreneur","prototype","launch","affordable","innovative","determined","tackle","profit","workshop","inspire","demand"],"b":["artificial intelligence","generate","algorithm","original","imitate","replace","impressive","threat","tool","debate","imagination","skill"],"box":["innovative","prototype","tackle","entrepreneur","launch","affordable","determined","profit","workshop","invention"],"gap":[["This clever, ___ design won a prize for its originality.","innovative"],["Before selling it, she tested the first ___ at home.","prototype"],["He wants to ___ the problem of plastic in the ocean.","tackle"],["A good ___ isn't afraid to start a new business.","entrepreneur"],["They will ___ their new gadget online next week.","launch"],["The tools are cheap and ___ for everyone.","affordable"],["She was so ___ that she never gave up.","determined"],["After paying all the costs, their ___ was small.","profit"],["Students build and repair things in the school ___.","workshop"],["The smartphone is probably the most important ___ of our time.","invention"]],"rt":"The Rise of Young Makers","rp":["Not long ago, turning an idea into a real product was expensive and slow. Today, thanks to cheap technology like 3D printers, almost anyone can become a maker. Around the world, more and more young people are designing inventions in their bedrooms and garages — and some are even becoming entrepreneurs and starting their own companies. Many young makers are motivated by everyday problems rather than complicated scientific questions. They often start small, using materials that are easy to find and learning as they go. For some, the experience is more important than creating a perfect product on the first try.","Take Leila, a seventeen-year-old from Manchester. When her grandmother struggled to open jars because of arthritis, Leila designed a simple plastic tool to help. She built her first prototype using a 3D printer in her school's workshop. At first, the tool was uncomfortable to hold, so she went back to her original design and made several small changes. After many failed attempts, she finally created something that worked. \"I was so determined to solve the problem,\" she says. \"I didn't give up.\" Her affordable invention is now sold online, and the demand keeps growing.","Others focus on protecting the planet. Diego, aged nineteen, noticed how much plastic his family threw away. He decided to tackle the problem by turning old bottles into innovative furniture. He launched a small business and began by making a few pieces for people in his local community. Gradually, his products attracted attention from other customers. \"I wanted to prove that being green can also be good business,\" he explains. His products are expensive to make, so his profit is still small — but he believes it will grow.","Experts say this \"maker movement\" is changing how young people think about work. Instead of waiting for the perfect job, they create their own. This approach encourages students to take more responsibility for their own ideas. They can decide what they want to explore and learn to deal with unexpected problems along the way. Schools are helping too, by opening workshops where students can build and experiment. \"Young makers aren't afraid to fail,\" one teacher notes. \"Every mistake teaches them something.\" Their experiences can also inspire other students to turn their own ideas into projects.","Of course, not every idea becomes successful. For some young makers, the biggest reward is not becoming famous or making a lot of money. It is seeing an idea develop from something on paper into something that people can actually use. But even those who fail learn valuable skills. As Leila puts it, \"You don't need to be a genius to be a maker. You just need a problem you really want to fix — and the patience to keep trying.\""],"rq":[{"q":"What is the text mainly about?","o":["Young people who are developing their own inventions and turning them into businesses","The steps involved in setting up a successful furniture business","The reasons why 3D printers remain expensive","The historical development of modern technology"],"a":0},{"q":"In the context of the text, the word “prototype” refers to:","o":["A facility where products are manufactured on a large scale","A copy of a well-known commercial product","An initial version of a product developed for testing and improvement","A detailed plan for establishing a new business"],"a":2},{"q":"Why did Leila design her tool?","o":["She needed to create an original product for a school examination.","She wanted to generate income as quickly as possible.","She wanted to find a way to reduce the amount of plastic people use.","She wanted to make it easier for her grandmother to open jars."],"a":3},{"q":"What can we understand about Diego's business?","o":["It has already become highly profitable.","It has a meaningful purpose, although it has not generated much profit so far.","It operates exclusively through online sales.","It has failed completely despite his efforts."],"a":1},{"q":"According to the text, how are schools helping?","o":["By providing students with financial support for their projects","By helping students find employment after graduation","By providing workshops where students can create, test, and experiment with their ideas","By marketing and selling students' inventions on their behalf"],"a":2}],"who":{"p":["Leila","Diego","Experts/Teacher"],"q":[["I wanted to show that being eco-friendly can be good business.","Diego"],["Young makers learn from every mistake.","Experts/Teacher"],["I refused to give up until it worked.","Leila"],["You just need a problem you really want to fix.","Leila"],["The maker movement is changing how young people see work.","Experts/Teacher"]]},"lq":[{"q":"What is the main question of the discussion?","o":["Whether machines can truly be creative","Whether digital art is better than traditional art","How artists can earn money from their work","Which technology is best for making music"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Dr Chen say AI produces art?","o":["It copies one famous work and changes its colours.","It learns the personal story of each artist.","It studies millions of existing works and makes something similar.","It follows instructions written by human artists."],"a":2},{"q":"What was Sara's first reaction to AI art?","o":["She was excited because it saved her time.","She was worried it might end human art.","She was disappointed by the poor quality.","She was angry with the researchers."],"a":1},{"q":"\"imitate\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["copy something closely","improve something carefully","invent something new","study something in detail"],"a":0},{"q":"What do both guests seem to believe by the end?","o":["AI will slowly replace creative jobs.","Artists should avoid using AI tools.","AI art is more valuable than human art.","Humans and AI can work well together."],"a":3}],"lwho":{"p":["Sara","Dr Chen"],"q":[["AI is clever imitation, but it has no feelings or story.","Dr Chen"],["The final emotion in my art still comes from me.","Sara"],["New technology usually creates new opportunities too.","Dr Chen"],["Artists who succeed will learn to use these tools.","Sara"],["AI will change how designers work, not replace them.","Dr Chen"]]},"au":"audio/u2.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"In the text, \"affordable\" means:","o":["cheap enough for many people to buy","simple enough for children to use","made from materials that are easy to find","designed to last for many years"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did Leila make changes to her first tool?","o":["It was uncomfortable to hold.","It broke when her grandmother used it.","The 3D printer in the workshop stopped working.","It was too expensive to make at school."],"a":0},{"q":"Why is Diego's profit still small?","o":["He has only just started selling online.","Local customers prefer cheaper furniture.","His products are expensive to make.","He has to pay for a large workshop."],"a":2},{"q":"Where did Diego begin selling his furniture?","o":["Through an online shop","At a school market","In a large furniture store","In his local community"],"a":3},{"q":"What does the teacher's comment about mistakes suggest?","o":["Young makers make fewer mistakes than adults.","Schools should avoid difficult projects.","Mistakes make products more expensive.","Young makers learn from failure."],"a":3},{"q":"What is the biggest reward for some young makers?","o":["Earning enough money to start a company","Being praised by teachers and experts","Seeing their idea become something people can use","Winning a competition for young inventors"],"a":2}],"lq2":[{"q":"How long did the AI take to produce the painting Sara watched?","o":["About 30 seconds","About three minutes","About 30 minutes","About a day"],"a":0},{"q":"What did Sara feel was missing in the AI images?","o":["Beautiful colours","Fine detail","A clear style","Real meaning"],"a":3},{"q":"How could a designer use AI, according to Dr Chen?","o":["To create many rough ideas quickly","To make the final creative decisions","To replace the other designers in a team","To check the quality of finished work"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Sara use AI in her own work?","o":["To generate rough ideas when she feels stuck","To create the final emotion of her artwork","To sell her work online","To study other artists' styles"],"a":0},{"q":"Which skills does Dr Chen think will become more valuable?","o":["Speed and accuracy","Technical drawing and painting","Imagination and storytelling","Knowledge of AI programming"],"a":2},{"q":"What does \"The camera didn't kill painting\" mean?","o":["Cameras made painting much cheaper to learn.","Painters refused to use new technology.","New technology can change an art without ending it.","Photography became less popular than painting."],"a":2}],"ph":[{"q":"Our invention _____ the problem of plastic waste.","o":["tackles","makes","builds","tries"],"a":0},{"q":"AI could help us _____ giving us new ideas.","o":["by","for","at","in"],"a":0},{"q":"We could use AI _____ create rough sketches.","o":["to","by","for","at"],"a":0},{"q":"I don't think AI _____ make the final decision.","o":["should","to","is","for"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u3","n":3,"t":"Challenges","a":["resilience","setback","overcome","failure","bounce back","cope","persevere","mindset","obstacle","give up","confidence","motivation"],"b":["adapt","adjust","challenge","routine","homesick","unfamiliar","gradually","support","comfort zone","struggle","get used to","independent"],"box":["setback","mindset","overcome","persevere","give up","obstacle","resilience","cope","failure","bounce back"],"gap":[["Losing all his money was a huge ___, but he didn't quit.","setback"],["A positive ___ helps you see problems as lessons.","mindset"],["She managed to ___ her fear and speak on stage.","overcome"],["If you ___, you'll finish the project eventually.","persevere"],["Never just ___ because something is hard.","give up"],["The main ___ to her success was a lack of time.","obstacle"],["People with ___ recover quickly from difficulties.","resilience"],["He learned to ___ with pressure by taking breaks.","cope"],["One ___ is not the end of the world.","failure"],["Good athletes always ___ after a bad game.","bounce back"]],"rt":"Bouncing Back: The Power of Resilience","rp":["Everyone experiences failure at some point. We fail a test, lose a game, or make a mistake at work. What matters, psychologists say, is not the failure itself but how we respond to it. This ability to recover from difficulties is called resilience — and the good news is that it can be learned. People can face very different kinds of difficulties in their personal, academic, or professional lives. What feels like a small problem to one person may be much more difficult for another.","Consider Maya, a long-distance runner. Two years ago, a serious injury stopped her from competing, and doctors said she might never run again. \"It was the biggest setback of my life,\" she remembers. \"For weeks I wanted to give up.\" But instead of quitting, she slowly rebuilt her strength and worked hard to overcome her injury. Her experience changed the way she thought about her goals, and she began to see progress differently. Last spring, she finished her first marathon since the injury. \"The pain taught me patience,\" she says.","Failure can also lead to success in business. When Tom opened his first café, it closed within a year. He had lost his savings and felt like a complete failure. However, he refused to let one mistake define him. The experience stayed with him for a long time, and he thought carefully about what he had learned from it. He studied what went wrong, changed his plan, and opened a second café. Today it is one of the most popular in his city. \"Every obstacle taught me something useful,\" he explains. His experience also gave him the motivation to try again.","Experts believe that a positive mindset is the key. People with resilience don't see failure as the end; they see it as a lesson. They understand that difficult experiences can affect their confidence and emotions, but they try to keep these experiences in perspective. They also know how to cope with stress by asking for help, staying hopeful, and focusing on small steps forward. People who persevere through difficult periods are often better prepared to bounce back from future challenges.","Building resilience takes time and practice. It can develop gradually as people experience challenges and learn more about how they respond to them. The next time you face a difficult moment, remember Maya and Tom. Success is rarely a straight line. Often, the people who achieve the most are simply the ones who refused to give up trying."],"rq":[{"q":"What is the writer's main message?","o":["Failure is something that should be avoided at all costs.","How we respond to failure matters more than the failure itself","Resilience is a quality that can only be developed by professional athletes.","Taking up running is one of the best ways to maintain good health."],"a":1},{"q":"In the context of the text, the word “setback” is closest in meaning to:","o":["an unexpected success or achievement","a challenging event in which people compete against each other","a difficulty that prevents or delays further progress","a choice made after carefully considering different possibilities"],"a":2},{"q":"What did Maya do after her injury?","o":["She decided to give up running permanently after her injury.","She decided to study medicine and become a doctor.","She changed careers and opened a café instead.","She gradually regained her strength and eventually completed a marathon."],"a":3},{"q":"What can we understand about Tom?","o":["He had never experienced failure or made any significant mistakes.","He learned from his initial failure and was willing to make another attempt.","He failed to keep his second café open and eventually closed it as well.","He had little or no interest in running a business in the first place."],"a":1},{"q":"According to experts, how do resilient people cope with stress?","o":["Ignoring their difficulties until they eventually disappear","Giving up when a situation becomes too challenging to handle","Seeking support from others and focusing on manageable steps","Dealing with their problems independently without relying on others"],"a":2}],"who":{"p":["Maya","Tom","Experts"],"q":[["Difficulty taught me to be patient.","Maya"],["Resilient people treat failure as a lesson, not an ending.","Experts"],["I studied my mistake and changed my plan.","Tom"],["Doctors thought I might never do my sport again.","Maya"],["Recovering from problems is a skill you can learn.","Experts"]]},"lq":[{"q":"Where did Aylin move?","o":["To Canada (from Turkey)","To Turkey (from Canada)","To a huge city (from a tiny village)","To the UK (from Turkey)"],"a":0},{"q":"Where did Marco move?","o":["To another country (from his home city)","To Canada (from Turkey)","To a huge city (from a tiny village)","To a tiny village (from a huge city)"],"a":2},{"q":"What are Aylin and Marco mainly talking about?","o":["Why students leave university early","How they adapted to a major change","How to choose a university abroad","Ways to make friends online"],"a":1},{"q":"How did Aylin start to make friends?","o":["By calling her family every day","By joining a photography club","By taking a language class","By sharing a flat with other students"],"a":1},{"q":"What helped Marco feel more in control?","o":["Moving to a quieter part of the city","Avoiding the noisy crowds in the morning","Talking to his family every evening","Creating a regular daily routine"],"a":3},{"q":"\"homesick\" means:","o":["feeling tired after a long journey to a new place","feeling sad because you are away from home","feeling nervous because you cannot speak the language","feeling lonely because you have no friends"],"a":1},{"q":"What do both students agree about?","o":["It is better to solve problems on your own.","Moving abroad is easier than moving within a country.","Joining clubs is the best way to adapt.","Having support from others can make change easier."],"a":3}],"lwho":{"p":["Aylin","Marco"],"q":[["Moving to another country left me feeling homesick.","Aylin"],["Following a daily routine gave me a stronger sense of control.","Marco"],["Joining a club turned out to be a turning point for me.","Aylin"],["Being too shy to ask questions made the situation more difficult.","Marco"],["The move helped me become much more independent.","Marco"]]},"au":"audio/u3.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"According to the text, what is resilience?","o":["The ability to avoid all difficult situations","The determination to win every competition","The habit of learning from other people's mistakes","The ability to recover from difficulties"],"a":3},{"q":"What does the writer say about problems?","o":["Most people struggle with the same kinds of problems.","Personal problems are more serious than academic ones.","Difficult experiences become easier as people get older.","What seems small to one person may be very difficult for another."],"a":3},{"q":"What did Maya learn from her pain?","o":["To value winning more than taking part","To avoid risks in the future","To train less often than before","To be patient"],"a":3},{"q":"What happened to Tom's first café?","o":["It lost money for two years before closing.","It was sold because of a family problem.","It closed within a year.","It was successful until a new café opened nearby."],"a":2},{"q":"What did Tom do after his first café closed?","o":["He opened a second café.","He worked in another café to save money.","He spent a year training as a chef.","He asked friends to help him start a new job."],"a":0},{"q":"In the text, \"persevere\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["wait patiently until problems disappear","continue trying although it is difficult","try a different method immediately","accept a result without complaining"],"a":1}],"lq2":[{"q":"How often did Aylin call her family in her first month?","o":["Twice a week","Only at weekends","Every day","Once a month"],"a":2},{"q":"What was the turning point for Aylin?","o":["Joining a photography club","Moving to a new flat","Calling her family","Starting a new course"],"a":0},{"q":"Where did Marco grow up?","o":["In a small coastal town","In a tiny village","In a huge city","In another country"],"a":1},{"q":"What made even taking the bus stressful for Marco?","o":["The buses were always late.","He kept getting lost on the routes.","Everything was fast and noisy.","He was afraid of travelling alone."],"a":2},{"q":"Who became a great source of support for Marco?","o":["His teacher","His roommate","His older brother","A counsellor at university"],"a":1},{"q":"What advice does Aylin give at the start of her story?","o":["Join as many clubs as possible","Step out of your comfort zone","Call your family every day","Wait until you feel comfortable"],"a":1}],"ph":[{"q":"Our first _____ is to join a club.","o":["tip","tipping","tips of","tipped"],"a":0},{"q":"They should _____ to ask for help early.","o":["try","trying","tried","tries"],"a":0},{"q":"This can help them adjust _____ they will meet new people.","o":["because","but","although","unless"],"a":0},{"q":"We think this is more important _____ making a perfect plan.","o":["than","that","then","as"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u4","n":4,"t":"Science","a":["device","monitor","sensor","data","detect","accurate","diagnose","treatment","prevent","reliable","breakthrough","wearable"],"b":["renewable","fossil fuels","emissions","sustainable","solar power","efficient","resource","reduce","pollution","alternative","invest","carbon footprint"],"box":["breakthrough","monitor","data","sensor","diagnose","accurate","detect","treatment","prevent","device"],"gap":[["A new medical ___ could change how we treat illness.","breakthrough"],["My watch can ___ my heart rate 24 hours a day.","monitor"],["The app collects a lot of personal ___.","data"],["A tiny ___ inside the watch measures movement.","sensor"],["Doctors could not ___ the illness until it was serious.","diagnose"],["Good technology should be exact and ___.","accurate"],["This tool can ___ problems you cannot feel.","detect"],["Early care means better ___ for patients.","treatment"],["Healthy habits can ___ many diseases.","prevent"],["A smartphone is the most common ___ in the world.","device"]],"rt":"Health at Your Wrist","rp":["A few years ago, a watch could only tell you the time. Today, many watches can do much more. Modern wearable devices can monitor your heart rate, count your steps, check your sleep, and even measure the oxygen in your blood. For millions of people, health information is now available right on their wrist. People use these devices for different reasons. Some want to become more active, while others simply enjoy having information about their daily habits. As the technology becomes more common, wearable technology is becoming a familiar part of everyday life.","These devices use tiny sensors to collect data about the body. Some can detect problems that the wearer cannot feel. Take David, a 45-year-old teacher. One evening, his smartwatch warned him that his heart rhythm was unusual. At first, he ignored it, but the warning continued. When he finally visited a hospital, doctors discovered a serious heart condition. \"That little device probably saved my life,\" he says. The experience changed the way David thought about the information on his wrist. He had never expected a small device to provide information that could be useful in such an important situation.","Doctors are becoming more interested in this technology too. Dr Silva, a heart specialist, explains that wearables allow patients to be monitored at home instead of in hospital. \"We can now collect information over weeks, not just during a short visit,\" she says. \"This helps us diagnose problems earlier and choose better treatment.\" For doctors, having access to information collected during normal daily life can provide a different picture from what they see during a single appointment. This may be especially useful when symptoms do not appear every day.","However, experts also warn that the technology is not perfect. The results are not always accurate, and a watch is not a replacement for a real doctor. Some people worry about privacy, because these devices collect huge amounts of personal data. \"Wearables are a useful tool,\" one researcher notes, \"but they should support medical care, not replace it.\" Users also need to understand that different devices may collect and present information in different ways. Learning how to use the technology correctly is therefore becoming an important part of using wearables responsibly.","Scientists believe this is only the beginning. In the future, wearable devices may be able to detect illnesses even earlier and help prevent them. This kind of breakthrough could change medicine forever. Researchers are also exploring new ways to make these devices smaller, more comfortable, and easier to use. As the technology develops, the amount of information collected from the human body may continue to grow. The dream is simple: to keep people healthy before they ever become sick."],"rq":[{"q":"What is the article mainly about?","o":["The most affordable ways to purchase wearable technology","The ways in which wearable devices can contribute to healthcare and medical treatment","The reasons why doctors may no longer be necessary in the future","The historical development of clocks and timekeeping devices"],"a":1},{"q":"In the context of the article, the word “monitor” is closest in meaning to:","o":["to offer something for sale","to keep something out of sight or prevent it from being noticed","to damage something so that it no longer works","to observe and check something regularly over a period of time"],"a":3},{"q":"What did David's smartwatch do?","o":["It only kept track of the number of steps he took each day.","It detected an unusual heart rhythm and alerted him to a possible health problem.","It automatically contacted a doctor on his behalf.","It suddenly stopped functioning while he was wearing it."],"a":1},{"q":"Why are doctors like Dr Silva interested in wearables?","o":["They believe wearable technology could eventually eliminate the need to see patients.","They are interested in wearables mainly because they consider smartwatches fashionable.","Wearables can provide health data over extended periods, potentially allowing doctors to identify problems at an earlier stage.","They believe wearable devices could encourage patients to remain in hospital for longer"],"a":2},{"q":"What warning do experts give?","o":["The information provided by wearable devices is unreliable.","The results are not always accurate and shouldn't replace a doctor.","People should avoid using wearable devices for health-related purposes altogether.","Wearable devices are too cheap to provide trustworthy health information."],"a":1}],"who":{"p":["David","Dr Silva","Experts/Researcher"],"q":[["This kind of device probably saved my life.","David"],["Wearables should support medical care, not replace it.","Experts/Researcher"],["We can now collect data over weeks, not just one visit.","Dr Silva"],["At first, I ignored the warning.","David"],["People should be careful about their personal data.","Experts/Researcher"]]},"lq":[{"q":"Who is more optimistic about renewable energy at the start of the discussion?","o":["Paul Grant","Both of them equally","Dr Osei","The radio presenter"],"a":2},{"q":"What is the main question of the discussion?","o":["Replacing fossil fuels with renewable energy","Making solar panels cheaper for families","Reducing the use of electricity in homes","Deciding which countries pollute the most"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Dr Osei say about solar power today?","o":["It is now one of the cheapest energy sources.","It is still more expensive than wind power.","It already supplies most of the world's electricity.","It works well even on cloudy, calm days."],"a":0},{"q":"What is Paul's main concern?","o":["The danger of building wind turbines","Public lack of interest in clean energy","The high cost of making the change","The damage that solar farms cause to nature"],"a":2},{"q":"\"invest\" means:","o":["To spend money on something you need immediately","To lend money to someone for a short time","To put money in now to gain something later","To save money in a bank for emergencies"],"a":2},{"q":"What do both guests finally agree on?","o":["Renewable energy can replace fossil fuels within ten years.","Renewable energy will play an important role in the future.","Governments should pay for all energy changes.","Batteries are the only problem left to solve."],"a":1}],"lwho":{"p":["Dr Osei","Paul"],"q":[["Solar power has become one of the cheapest energy sources.","Dr Osei"],["On a cloudy, calm day, we still need backup power.","Paul"],["Not changing will be even more expensive in the end.","Dr Osei"],["Building all the new equipment costs a great deal of money.","Paul"],["Governments investing properly could make change happen faster.","Dr Osei"]]},"au":"audio/u4.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"Which of these can modern wearable devices do, according to the text?","o":["Predict illnesses several years in advance","Diagnose serious diseases without a doctor","Measure the oxygen in the air around you","Monitor your heart rate and count your steps"],"a":3},{"q":"How did David react to the first warning from his smartwatch?","o":["He contacted his doctor the same evening.","He turned off the notification feature.","He ignored it at first.","He asked a friend to check the watch."],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Dr Silva think wearables are useful?","o":["They help patients choose which hospital to visit.","They let doctors collect information over weeks.","They allow doctors to treat patients without appointments.","They are more accurate than hospital equipment."],"a":1},{"q":"Why are some people worried about wearables?","o":["The devices send warnings that scare users.","The devices cost too much for most users.","The devices collect huge amounts of personal data.","The devices stop working after a few months."],"a":2},{"q":"In the last paragraph, \"breakthrough\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["a gradual improvement over many years","a serious mistake in research","an important new discovery","a type of modern medical equipment"],"a":2},{"q":"What is the dream of scientists mentioned in the text?","o":["To make hospital visits shorter","To keep people healthy before they become sick","To give every person a smartwatch","To replace general doctors with sensors"],"a":1}],"lq2":[{"q":"How long ago, according to Dr Osei, was solar power expensive and rare?","o":["5 years","10 years","20 years","2 years"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Paul say we need until energy can be stored cheaply?","o":["Larger solar farms in sunny regions","Other sources as backup","Wind turbines in every city","More power lines between countries"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Dr Osei say fossil fuels create?","o":["Huge amounts of emissions that harm the planet","Large amounts of waste that is hard to store","Noise that affects wildlife","Heat that damages power lines"],"a":0},{"q":"Why is the change a real challenge for poorer countries?","o":["They receive less sun than richer countries.","Their governments refuse to use new technology.","They cannot buy the batteries they need from abroad.","Someone has to invest money now, although the benefits come later."],"a":3},{"q":"Why does Dr Osei say that not changing is expensive?","o":["Pollution causes health problems and climate change causes floods and droughts.","Solar farms need expensive repairs every year.","Fossil fuels will soon cost much more than renewables.","Power lines are costly to remove."],"a":0},{"q":"Which of these can ordinary people do, according to the guests?","o":["Choose green energy suppliers","Install a wind turbine at home","Use electricity mainly at night","Buy batteries to store energy"],"a":0}],"ph":[{"q":"I wouldn't _____ on the smartwatch because it isn't always accurate.","o":["rely","relay","relate","remind"],"a":0},{"q":"I'd check the information _____ before trusting it.","o":["first","firstly of","one","early of"],"a":0},{"q":"One major _____ is that electric cars produce no emissions.","o":["advantage","advantages is","advance","advertise"],"a":0},{"q":"I'd probably buy a cheaper device _____ I don't need extra features.","o":["because","although","unless","so that"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u5","n":5,"t":"Explore","a":["destination","authentic","overcrowded","impact","preserve","resident","remote","souvenir","respect","thoughtful","consequence","mindset"],"b":["solo","adventure","freedom","spontaneous","nervous","accommodation","budget","get around","rewarding","stranger","journey","broaden your horizons"],"box":["overcrowded","preserve","impact","remote","thoughtful","souvenir","destination","residents","respect","authentic"],"gap":[["The beach was so ___ that we couldn't find anywhere to sit.","overcrowded"],["Buying local products helps ___ traditional skills.","preserve"],["Tourism can have a huge ___ on nature and on prices.","impact"],["They travelled to a ___ village that few tourists ever reach.","remote"],["A ___ traveller thinks about the effect of their choices.","thoughtful"],["I bought a small ___ to remember the trip.","souvenir"],["Barcelona is a world-famous holiday ___.","destination"],["Local ___ sometimes struggle to enjoy their own city.","residents"],["We should always ___ the customs of the places we visit.","respect"],["She wanted a real, ___ experience, not a tourist show.","authentic"]],"rt":"Travel That Gives Back: The Quiet Rise of Responsible Tourism","rp":["Every summer, millions of people head for the same famous destinations. Photographs on social media persuade thousands to book the same trip, resulting in a problem called \"overtourism\". In popular places, streets become overcrowded, house prices rise, and the beauty that first attracted visitors begins to disappear. As more people travel around the world, many destinations are finding it difficult to manage the growing number of visitors. The problem is not simply the number of tourists, but also the way they travel and the pressure their choices can place on a destination.","In recent years, however, a quieter style of travel has been growing. Called responsible, or sustainable, tourism, its central idea is simple: to explore the world without damaging it. Rather than treating a country as a backdrop for photographs, responsible travellers try to protect the environment and support local communities. This approach encourages visitors to think about the effect of their choices before and during a trip. It also asks travellers to see themselves as guests rather than simply consumers, and to consider how their presence affects the places they visit.","Emma, a young traveller from Ireland, shows how attitudes are changing. A few years ago, she used to rush from one famous landmark to the next, staying just long enough to take a picture. Today, she seeks out smaller, remote villages. \"I want an authentic experience,\" she explains. \"I stay in family-run guesthouses and eat food cooked by local people.\" For Emma, this new approach has also changed the way she plans her trips. She now spends more time researching places before she visits them and chooses accommodation based on what kind of experience she wants.","Local businesses also benefit. Mr. Costa, who runs a small guesthouse in northern Portugal, says these travellers stay longer, eat in village cafés, and hire local guides. This supports local families and helps preserve their way of life. He believes that visitors can have a positive role when they spend their money in ways that connect them with the local community. For small businesses, these choices can make tourism feel more personal and meaningful.","Experts argue that small decisions—like choosing trains over planes or buying directly from local artists—can reduce negative impacts. \"You don't have to stop travelling,\" one researcher points out. \"The goal is simply to travel a little more thoughtfully than in the past.\" There is no single perfect way to travel responsibly, and different destinations may have different needs. What matters is becoming more aware of the choices we make and their possible consequences. Responsible tourism is ultimately about a change in mindset and genuine respect for local communities."],"rq":[{"q":"What is the writer's main purpose?","o":["To discourage people from travelling abroad altogether","To describe a more thoughtful style of travel that protects places and communities","To promote small villages in Portugal and Ireland as tourist destinations","To explain how tourists can take more impressive holiday photographs"],"a":1},{"q":"According to the text, \"overtourism\" leads to all of the following EXCEPT:","o":["Local residents struggling to move freely through their neighborhoods","Housing becoming increasingly unaffordable for ordinary families","The gradual loss of the beauty of the places that attract tourists","A growing number of employment opportunities for local tour guides"],"a":3},{"q":"What can we infer about how Emma used to travel?","o":["She avoided taking photographs at popular tourist attractions.","She focused on quickly seeing famous sights rather than understanding places.","She preferred staying in expensive hotels rather than exploring local communities.","She was unwilling to travel to countries outside her own."],"a":1},{"q":"The phrase \"treating a country as a backdrop for photographs\" implies that some tourists:","o":["regard unfamiliar places as potentially dangerous and avoid exploring them","view destinations mainly as attractive settings for photographs rather than places to understand and experience","choose destinations primarily because they offer luxurious and expensive experiences","are more interested in using foreign destinations for business purposes"],"a":1},{"q":"Which sentence best expresses the researcher's opinion?","o":["Travellers should feel guilty whenever they choose to take a holiday.","International travel should be significantly reduced because of its environmental impact.","People can continue travelling as long as they make more thoughtful and responsible choices.","Travelling by train is the only acceptable option for environmentally responsible tourists."],"a":2}],"who":{"p":["Emma","Mr Costa","Researcher/Experts","Critics"],"q":[["Some people use \"responsible tourism\" just to feel good about themselves.","Critics"],["You see a completely different side of a country when you slow down.","Emma"],["Visitors now buy bread from the baker and hire local guides.","Mr Costa"],["The aim isn't to feel guilty — it's to travel with greater awareness and thoughtfulness.","Researcher/Experts"],["This way of travelling offers a richer experience for visitors while benefiting the local community.","Emma"]]},"lq":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the radio segment?","o":["To explain how to plan a cheap trip abroad","To share two travellers' honest experiences of going alone","To show why solo travel is better than group travel","To persuade older people to start travelling"],"a":1},{"q":"How did Priya feel before her first solo trip?","o":["Mildly worried about the budget","Embarrassed to tell her family","Terrified","Unsure which countries to visit"],"a":2},{"q":"What is Robert's attitude towards travelling alone at his age?","o":["He enjoys it but finds it too tiring at his age.","He travels alone only because he lost his friends.","He loves it and feels it has given him adventure again.","He thinks it is slightly strange but accepts it."],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Robert mention staying in small guesthouses?","o":["Because he meets other travellers there, so loneliness never lasts long","Because they let him save money for longer trips","Because the owners often show him walking routes","Because they are quieter than big hotels in the evening"],"a":0},{"q":"When Priya says every problem she solved \"made me more confident\", she means that:","o":["facing and solving difficulties increased her self-belief","she learned to avoid situations that cause stress","she found that most problems solved themselves","she decided to travel with someone next time"],"a":0}],"lwho":{"p":["Priya","Robert"],"q":[["I'd never even eaten in a restaurant alone before my trip.","Priya"],["Some people think it's strange to travel solo at my age.","Robert"],["I've learned to enjoy my own company.","Robert"],["Solving each problem made me more confident.","Priya"],["Start small and build up — you're braver than you think.","Priya"]]},"au":"audio/u5.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"What is \"overtourism\"?","o":["Spending too much money while on holiday","Travelling too often for short trips","Visiting the same famous place every year","Too many tourists in popular places, causing problems"],"a":3},{"q":"What is the central idea of responsible tourism?","o":["To spend as little money as possible while travelling","To visit only places that are free to enter","To explore the world without damaging it","To avoid travelling to other countries"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Emma do now when she travels?","o":["She stays in cheap hostels close to landmarks.","She joins organised tours of famous places.","She stays in family-run guesthouses in smaller villages.","She rents a house in a big city for a month."],"a":2},{"q":"How does Mr Costa say responsible visitors help local people?","o":["They pay higher prices for rooms in the guesthouse.","They bring their own food and equipment.","They help to repair old village buildings.","They stay longer, eat in village cafés and hire local guides."],"a":3},{"q":"In Emma's words, \"authentic\" means:","o":["real and true, not made for tourists","unusual and surprising for foreigners","traditional but extremely expensive","simple and cheap to organise"],"a":0},{"q":"Which small decision do experts mention?","o":["Visiting only during the winter months","Choosing trains over planes","Staying in large, modern hotels","Booking trips well in advance"],"a":1}],"lq2":[{"q":"How long was Priya's solo trip in Southeast Asia?","o":["Six weeks","Three months","Two months","Two weeks"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Priya like most about travelling alone?","o":["The freedom to do exactly what she wants","The chance to save money on food","Meeting people from her own country","Not having to plan anything"],"a":0},{"q":"Why does Robert travel alone?","o":["He is retired and his friends prefer to stay at home.","He is retired and has travelled alone since his wife passed away.","He wanted to save money after he stopped working.","He started travelling alone after a doctor's advice."],"a":1},{"q":"Why is solo travel more expensive, according to Priya?","o":["Flights for one person cost more.","Local guides charge higher prices to single travellers.","Restaurants add extra fees for one person.","You can't share the cost of a room."],"a":3},{"q":"How does Priya try to stay safe?","o":["She stays in large hotels and avoids small towns.","She shares her location with other travellers.","She avoids going out after dark.","She tells her family her plans and trusts her instincts."],"a":3},{"q":"What advice do they give to nervous travellers?","o":["Travel with a friend on the first trip","Start small, for example with a weekend somewhere close","Choose a country where you speak the language","Plan every day of the trip in detail"],"a":1}],"ph":[{"q":"We're _____ to visit a small village in the mountains.","o":["planning","plan","planned","plans"],"a":0},{"q":"A big advantage _____ that it is cheap.","o":["is","are","has","does"],"a":0},{"q":"Solo travel is exciting. _____ the other hand, it can be lonely.","o":["On","In","At","By"],"a":0},{"q":"If I _____ in this situation, I'd call my family first.","o":["were","am","will be","would be"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u6","n":6,"t":"Goodness","a":["volunteer","charity","generous","donate","community","cause","selfless","fulfilling","spare","gratitude","willing","encourage"],"b":["waste","leftovers","edible","surplus","expiry date","redistribute","campaign","combat","packaging","portion","household","throw away"],"box":["volunteer","donate","cause","fulfilling","selfless","spare","community","charity","gratitude","generous"],"gap":[["Every summer she gives her time to ___ at an animal shelter.","volunteer"],["They decided to ___ their old furniture instead of selling it.","donate"],["Protecting the environment is a ___ worth supporting.","cause"],["Helping others is one of the most ___ things you can do.","fulfilling"],["It was a ___ act — she asked for nothing in return.","selfless"],["Can you ___ ten minutes to help me carry these?","spare"],["The whole ___ came together to rebuild the park.","community"],["He works for a ___ that provides food to families in need.","charity"],["The old man's eyes filled with ___ when we helped.","gratitude"],["Thank you — that was incredibly ___ of you.","generous"]],"rt":"Small Acts, Big Difference","rp":["We often imagine that changing the world requires great wealth, power or talent. Yet a growing body of research suggests that some of the most meaningful differences are made by ordinary people performing small, everyday acts of kindness. Whether cleaning a local park, choosing to donate unwanted clothes, or helping a stranger carry heavy bags, these actions add up — and, surprisingly, they benefit the giver just as much as the receiver.","Sofia, a nineteen-year-old student, discovered this by accident. Feeling lonely after moving to a new city, she signed up to volunteer at a centre for elderly people. She expected it to be a chore, but instead it changed her life. \"I made friends, I felt useful, and for the first time in months I wasn't thinking only about my own problems,\" she says. Volunteering provided a selfless and fulfilling sense of purpose. Sofia still volunteers every week, encouraging others with her simple lesson: \"You don't need to be rich or famous to make someone's day better. You just need to care, and to show up.\"","Mr Adeyemi, who runs a community charity, has watched hundreds of volunteers pass through his doors. He argues that kindness is a skill that grows with practice. \"When people first arrive, many are shy and unsure,\" he explains. \"But after a few weeks they become more confident and more generous.\" He believes that strong communities are built not by governments alone, but by neighbours who are willing to spare a little of their time.","Scientists who study human behaviour agree that kindness has powerful effects. Psychologists have found that helping others can reduce stress, lower feelings of loneliness, and even improve physical health. When we do something generous, the brain releases chemicals that cause a \"helper's high\", leaving volunteers with profound gratitude. One psychologist notes, \"Kindness feels good because, throughout human history, it has helped us survive.\"","Critics point out that a single donated coat will not end poverty, arguing that serious problems need serious solutions from those in power. Supporters respond that small actions and big changes are not enemies, as every successful campaign began with individuals acting for a good cause. Furthermore, a single kind gesture can spread through a community like a ripple across water, touching people the original giver will never even meet. Waiting for someone else to fix the world usually means nothing changes at all."],"rq":[{"q":"The main idea of the text is that …","o":["Only wealthy and powerful people have the ability to make a real difference.","Small acts of kindness bring positive benefits to both the helper and the receiver.","Volunteering in community projects is usually a disappointing and exhausting task.","Scientific research shows that helping others has no real impact on society."],"a":1},{"q":"What can we infer about Sofia when she first started volunteering?","o":["She was a confident person who easily made new friends in the city.","She decided to volunteer mainly to earn money.","She already had a lot of experience working with elderly people.","She was feeling isolated and did not expect the experience to be very rewarding."],"a":3},{"q":"The phrase \"helper's high\" refers to …","o":["the financial cost and time needed to participate in voluntary work.","a special medical treatment used by doctors to reduce stress levels.","the positive emotional and physical feeling people get from being generous","a dangerous activity that volunteers should avoid in their daily lives."],"a":2},{"q":"Which sentence best expresses what supporters say to the critics?","o":["They argue that personal actions are useless unless the government gets involved.","They point out that major positive changes often start with caring individuals.","They believe that only large international charities can fix social problems.","They suggest that ordinary citizens should wait for political leaders to act."],"a":1}],"who":{"p":["Sofia","Mr Adeyemi","Psychologist","Critics"],"q":[["Big social problems need serious solutions from powerful leaders, not just small individual actions.","Critics"],["Being kind is like a skill that improves the more you practice it in daily life.","Mr Adeyemi"],["You do not need money or fame to help others; you just need to care and be there for them.","Sofia"],["Helping others feels good today because cooperation was necessary for human survival in the past.","Psychologist"]]},"tf":[["Sofia decided to give up volunteering immediately after her very first experience at the elderly centre.","False"],["Mr Adeyemi considers kindness to be a practical skill that develops as people gain more experience.","True"],["Scientific research confirms that offering help to others can effectively decrease stress and social isolation.","True"],["Before managing the community charity, Mr Adeyemi spent several years working as a professional scientist.","Not Given"]],"lq":[{"q":"What is the programme mainly about?","o":["The process of setting up a café","The different ways in which people are trying to cut down on food waste","The origins and development of cooking","The various ways in which smartphones can be used"],"a":1},{"q":"According to the presenter, roughly how much of the world's food is wasted?","o":["about a tenth","about a quarter","about a third","about a half"],"a":2},{"q":"How does Hannah feel about the time she spends donating food?","o":["She regards it as an excessive amount of work.","She considers it a minimal effort for a big result, and wishes she'd started sooner.","She feels that getting involved was ultimately a regrettable decision.","She finds it virtually impossible to fit into her routine."],"a":1},{"q":"On Diego's app, what do customers receive?","o":["A complimentary lesson in cooking","An order of food delivered directly to their homes","A restaurant meal for which they pay the full price","A \"surprise bag\" containing unsold food, available at a considerably lower price"],"a":3},{"q":"What is Diego's most important point?","o":["Most food waste comes from ordinary homes, so habits must change.","Apps could potentially eliminate the problem of food waste completely.","Shops are the primary, and essentially the only, source of food waste.","Technology is of little or no use in tackling the problem of food waste."],"a":0},{"q":"When Hannah says \"best before\" doesn't mean \"dangerous after\", she means:","o":["Food should be considered unsafe once it has passed its best-before date.","Food can often remain perfectly safe to eat even after its best-before date has passed.","Food should always be thrown away as soon as it reaches its best-before date.","The meaning of the label is too unclear for consumers to understand."],"a":1}],"lwho":{"p":["Hannah","Diego"],"q":[["Don't be too proud to buy \"ugly\" fruit and vegetables.","Diego"],["If we can't sell it, we donate it.","Hannah"],["Most food waste comes from ordinary households.","Diego"],["It only takes me ten extra minutes a day.","Hannah"],["Learn to love your leftovers.","Hannah"]]},"au":"audio/u6.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"Which of these is an example of a small act of kindness in the text?","o":["Funding a new hospital for the city","Cleaning a local park","Starting a company that employs many people","Winning a national award for service"],"a":1},{"q":"Why did Sofia sign up to volunteer?","o":["Her university required her to complete volunteer hours.","She wanted to gain experience for a future career.","She felt lonely after moving to a new city.","A friend suggested it after she lost her job."],"a":2},{"q":"What does Mr Adeyemi believe about strong communities?","o":["They are built by governments working with charities.","They are built by neighbours willing to spare some of their time.","They are built by wealthy people who donate large sums.","They are built by experienced volunteers rather than beginners."],"a":1},{"q":"According to psychologists, helping others can:","o":["make people feel more competitive","reduce stress and loneliness","lower the need for sleep","help people earn more money"],"a":1},{"q":"In the text, \"spare\" in \"spare a little of their time\" means:","o":["to save for later use","to use carefully so it is not wasted","to give up unwillingly","to give because you can"],"a":3},{"q":"The phrase \"like a ripple across water\" suggests that kindness:","o":["weakens as it travels further","spreads and touches many people","affects only people close to the giver","is easy to see at first but soon forgotten"],"a":1}],"lq2":[{"q":"What did Hannah see every evening at closing time?","o":["Perfectly good food going into the rubbish","Customers complaining about prices","Staff taking leftover food home","Food being delivered too late"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Hannah do with the unsold food?","o":["She sells it at a lower price the next morning.","She gives it to her staff to take home.","She sends it to a food bank in another city.","She gives it to a local shelter."],"a":3},{"q":"How did Hannah feel about the rules at first?","o":["She was sure that the rules would stop her.","She worried that customers would complain.","She worried about the rules, safety and time.","She thought it would cost too much money."],"a":2},{"q":"What does Diego say about the number of meals his app has rescued in three years?","o":["Hundreds of thousands","Millions","Exactly one million","Several thousand"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Diego say about his app?","o":["It won't solve the problem alone; real change must happen in our homes.","It has already solved the problem in most cities.","It works best only in small shops and bakeries.","It needs more money before it can become useful."],"a":0},{"q":"What does Diego say about \"ugly\" fruit and vegetables?","o":["Ugly vegetables are healthier than perfect ones.","Ugly fruit is always cheaper than perfect fruit.","They should be sold in separate shops.","A bent carrot tastes the same as a straight one."],"a":3}],"ph":[{"q":"We _____ that many people throw away food.","o":["noticed","notice to","noticing","notices"],"a":0},{"q":"Our main goal _____ to reduce food waste.","o":["is","are","do","have"],"a":0},{"q":"To solve this problem, we _____ to start a campaign.","o":["want","wants","wanting","wanted of"],"a":0},{"q":"Volunteers can support each other _____ sharing tasks.","o":["by","at","in","for"],"a":0}]}];